@lesson vae
Q: What are the two terms of the VAE's ELBO?
* A reconstruction term and a KL term between the encoder distribution and the prior
- A classification loss and weight decay
- Two adversarial losses
- Cross-entropy and accuracy
> \(\mathbb E_q[\log p(\mathbf x\mid\mathbf z)]-D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q(\mathbf z\mid\mathbf x)\|p(\mathbf z))\).

Q: Why is the reparameterization trick needed?
* Sampling is not differentiable, but \(\mathbf z=\boldsymbol\mu+\boldsymbol\sigma\odot\boldsymbol\epsilon\) moves the randomness into an input
- To make the decoder smaller
- To avoid using a prior
- To speed up inference
> Gradients can then flow through \(\boldsymbol\mu\) and \(\boldsymbol\sigma\).

Q: The ELBO is always less than or equal to \(\log p(\mathbf x)\).
= true
> The gap is \(D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q(\mathbf z\mid\mathbf x)\|p(\mathbf z\mid\mathbf x))\ge0\).

Q: What is the KL term for a 1-D encoder output \(\mu=0, \sigma=1\) against the prior \(\mathcal N(0,1)\)?
= 0
> The distributions are identical, so the KL divergence is zero.

@lesson gan
Q: For a fixed generator, the optimal discriminator outputs…
* \(p_{\text{data}}(\mathbf x)/(p_{\text{data}}(\mathbf x)+p_g(\mathbf x))\)
- 0.5 everywhere
- \(p_g(\mathbf x)\)
- 1 for every input
> When \(p_g=p_{\text{data}}\) this equals 0.5 everywhere.

Q: What is mode collapse?
- The discriminator stops learning
* The generator produces only a few kinds of output
- The loss becomes NaN
- The data has too many classes
> It fools the discriminator with a narrow set of samples instead of covering the data distribution.

Q: Why use the non-saturating generator loss \(-\log D(G(\mathbf z))\)?
* It gives strong gradients early in training, when the discriminator easily rejects fakes
- It removes the need for a discriminator
- It is cheaper to compute
- It guarantees convergence
> The original minimax loss saturates and gives vanishing gradients at the start.

@lesson diffusion
Q: Which closed form lets diffusion training jump directly to any noise level?
* \(\mathbf x_t=\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}\mathbf x_0+\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\boldsymbol\epsilon\)
- \(\mathbf x_t=\mathbf x_0+t\)
- \(\mathbf x_t=\bar\alpha_t\mathbf x_0\)
- \(\mathbf x_t=\boldsymbol\epsilon\)
> Composing Gaussian steps gives one Gaussian, so no need to simulate every step during training.

Q: What does the network learn to predict in the standard DDPM objective?
- The clean image directly
* The noise that was added
- The timestep
- The class label
> Training is plain MSE regression on the noise, which is stable.

Q: What does a classifier-free guidance weight \(w\gt1\) do?
* Pushes samples to follow the prompt more strongly, at some cost in diversity
- Makes sampling faster
- Removes the need for text conditioning
- Adds more noise
> It extrapolates from the unconditional to the conditional prediction.

Q: Latent diffusion runs the diffusion process in the compressed latent space of an autoencoder.
= true
> Working on smaller latents makes high-resolution generation affordable.

@lesson flows
Q: What makes normalizing flows' likelihood exact?
* Invertible transformations and the change-of-variables formula with a tractable Jacobian determinant
- An adversarial discriminator
- A variational lower bound
- Sampling many times
> Coupling layers keep the determinant cheap.

Q: In flow matching with straight paths, what is the regression target for \(v_\theta(\mathbf x_t,t)\)?
- \(\mathbf x_0\)
- \(\mathbf x_1\)
* \(\mathbf x_1-\mathbf x_0\)
- \(t\)
> On the straight line \(\mathbf x_t=(1-t)\mathbf x_0+t\mathbf x_1\) the velocity is constant.

Q: How do token-based image generators work?
* A VQ-VAE turns images into discrete codes, and a Transformer models the codes like text
- They predict pixels with a GAN
- They sample from a Gaussian process
- They use k-means on pixels
> The same autoregressive recipe as language modelling.

@lesson exam
Q: Which family offers fast sampling and sharp images but unstable training?
- VAEs
* GANs
- Diffusion models
- Normalizing flows
> Adversarial training is powerful but prone to collapse and oscillation.

Q: Which models train with a simple MSE regression objective? (select all)
* DDPM noise prediction
* Flow matching
- GANs
- Autoregressive token models
> Token models use cross-entropy; GANs use an adversarial game.

Q: A VAE's samples look blurry. Which is a common explanation?
* A Gaussian decoder with MSE-like reconstruction averages over plausible outputs
- The KL term is too small
- The encoder has no parameters
- VAEs cannot generate images
> Averaging uncertain details produces blur; diffusion and GAN decoders avoid it.

Q: What does Euler integration of a learned velocity field from \(t=0\) to \(t=1\) do in flow matching?
* Transports noise samples to data samples
- Trains the model
- Computes the likelihood exactly
- Adds noise to data
> Sampling is solving the ODE \(d\mathbf x/dt=v_\theta\).
